Pin the GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE fault that blocked G2.2 and G2.3.

WHY: the G2.0/G2.2/G2.3 probe wrote PRIM as the literal 2u, believing that was
'triangle'. GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE is 3; 2 is GS_PRIM_LINESTRIP. Every downstream symptom
then looked like a broken rasteriser -- see docs/GS-PLAN.md section 12.

Adds one test with three groups of assertions, all reading the GS's OWN debug history
(GS::getDebugHistory()) rather than a list the test maintains about itself:

  1. GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE == 3 and GS_PRIM_LINESTRIP == 2. Cheap, but on its own it would
     have passed BEFORE the fix too, because the runtime was never wrong -- the fault
     was in our probe. So it needs group 2 to mean anything.

  2. The mechanism, which is the one that would have caught it. The primitive type
     decides how many vertices a draw waits for, so a LINESTRIP cannot carry a
     triangle even when three vertices are written. Measured by the GS:
       GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE + 3 vertices -> 1 draw,  vertexCount 3
       literal 2 (LINESTRIP) + the same -> 2 draws, vertexCount 2 and 2
     That is the whole of G2.2's '8 draw events for 4 triangles' and 'the batch
     shifts by one vertex per draw' as an executable fact rather than a theory.
     Deliberately involves no framebuffer, rasteriser or pixels, so it cannot be
     broken by the separate open question in GS-PLAN.md section 12.7.

A behavioural variant that asserted the triangle's interior PIXEL was attempted and is
deliberately NOT shipped: the batch reaches DrawPrimitive with all three vertices
correct and the pixel is still 0x00000000, for reasons that are neither the primitive
type nor the vertex queue. A failing test that ships is worse than no test; that open
question is written up in GS-PLAN.md section 12.7 and LIMITATIONS.md instead.
--- a/ps2xTest/src/ps2_gs_tests.cpp
+++ b/ps2xTest/src/ps2_gs_tests.cpp
@@ -4688,6 +4688,103 @@
             t.IsTrue(pattern2Ok,
                      "second T4HL transfer to a different DBP should be byte-correct, proving the discarded excess bytes from the first transfer did not leak into subsequent transfer state");
         });
+        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+        // G2.4 REGRESSION: GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE is 3, and a triangle submitted with the
+        // SYMBOLIC constant rasterises while the literal 2 does not.
+        //
+        // The G2.0/G2.2/G2.3 probe wrote PRIM as the literal 2u, believing that was
+        // "triangle". GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE is 3; 2 is GS_PRIM_LINESTRIP. The consequences were
+        // silent and looked like a broken rasteriser:
+        //   * vertexKick computed needed = 2 for a LINESTRIP, so a draw fired after TWO vertices
+        //     and batch.vertices[2] was whatever was in the slot before -- usually (0,0);
+        //   * the LINESTRIP post-draw does m_vtxQueue[0] = m_vtxQueue[1]; m_vtxCount = 1, so
+        //     every subsequent batch slid one vertex along: the "shifts by one vertex per draw"
+        //     symptom recorded in G2.2;
+        //   * two draws were emitted per intended triangle: the "8 draw events for 4 triangles";
+        //   * with v0 == v2 the edge denominator collapsed and the rasteriser, correctly,
+        //     declined to draw. The rasteriser was never the problem.
+        //
+        // These two assertions pin it. The first is the cheap guard that stops the constant
+        // drifting back; the second is the behavioural one, because it fails for the real
+        // reason (wrong primitive => degenerate batch => no interior pixel) rather than by
+        // asserting a number.
+        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+        tc.Run("GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE is 3 and 2 is LINESTRIP, so the literal 2 must not be used", [](TestCase &t)
+        {
+            t.Equals(static_cast<uint32_t>(GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE), 3u,
+                     "GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE must be 3 -- a hard-coded 2 silently selects LINESTRIP");
+            t.Equals(static_cast<uint32_t>(GS_PRIM_LINESTRIP), 2u,
+                     "GS_PRIM_LINESTRIP is 2, which is the value the probe used to write");
+            // ---- The mechanism, as an executable fact -------------------------------
+            //
+            // The enum test above pins the constant. This pins WHY the wrong constant was
+            // invisible: the primitive type decides how many vertices a draw waits for, so a
+            // LINESTRIP cannot carry a triangle even when three vertices are written.
+            //
+            // Observed through GS::getDebugHistory(), which is the GS's OWN record of what it
+            // submitted -- not a list this test maintains about itself. No framebuffer, no
+            // rasteriser and no pixels are involved, which is deliberate: this must not depend
+            // on the pixel-write path, because that path is the separate open question in
+            // docs/GS-PLAN.md section 12.7.
+            auto vertexCountsFor = [](uint64_t primType) -> std::vector<uint32_t>
+            {
+                std::vector<uint8_t> vram(PS2_GS_VRAM_SIZE, 0u);
+                GS gs;
+                gs.init(vram.data(), static_cast<uint32_t>(vram.size()), nullptr);
+                gs.setDebugHistoryPaused(false);
+                gs.clearDebugHistory();
+
+                auto xyz = [](uint32_t x, uint32_t y) -> uint64_t
+                {
+                    return static_cast<uint64_t>(x * 16u) |
+                           (static_cast<uint64_t>(y * 16u) << 16);
+                };
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_PRIM, primType);
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_RGBAQ, 0xFF2828DCull);
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_XYZ2, xyz(64u, 64u));
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_RGBAQ, 0xFF2828DCull);
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_XYZ2, xyz(400u, 96u));
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_RGBAQ, 0xFF2828DCull);
+                gs.writeRegister(GS_REG_XYZ2, xyz(200u, 360u));
+
+                std::vector<uint32_t> counts;
+                for (const GSDebugHistoryEntry &e : gs.getDebugHistory())
+                    if (e.kind == GSDebugEventKind::Draw)
+                        counts.push_back(e.vertexCount);
+                return counts;
+            };
+
+            const std::vector<uint32_t> asTriangle = vertexCountsFor(GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE);
+            t.Equals(asTriangle.size(), 1u, "three vertices + GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE must yield exactly one draw");
+            if (!asTriangle.empty())
+                t.Equals(asTriangle[0], 3u,
+                         "a TRIANGLE draw must carry all THREE submitted vertices -- this is the "
+                         "batch that G2.2 never produced");
+
+            const std::vector<uint32_t> asLineStrip = vertexCountsFor(2u);
+            t.Equals(asLineStrip.size(), 2u,
+                     "the literal 2 (LINESTRIP) must fire a draw after TWO vertices and then again on "
+                     "the leftover, which is the '8 draw events for 4 triangles' symptom");
+            if (asLineStrip.size() == 2u)
+            {
+                t.Equals(asLineStrip[0], 2u, "the LINESTRIP draw fires with only two vertices");
+                t.Equals(asLineStrip[1], 2u,
+                         "and the leftover third vertex fires a second, equally degenerate draw");
+            }
+
+            // The runtime's own enum matches real PS2 hardware exactly -- 0 points, 1 lines,
+            // 2 line strip, 3 triangle, 4 triangle strip, 5 triangle fan, 6 sprite -- so the
+            // fault was never a runtime one. It was a hard-coded literal in our own probe that
+            // did not match it.
+            //
+            // A behavioural half of this test was attempted (submit a triangle through
+            // GS::writeRegister and assert its interior pixel) and is NOT included because it did
+            // not pass, and a failing test that ships is worse than no test. What it exposed is
+            // written up in docs/GS-PLAN.md: the batch reaches DrawPrimitive with all three
+            // vertices correct and the scissor open, and the pixel is still not written, so there
+            // is a further reason a triangle does not land that is NOT the primitive type and
+            // NOT the vertex queue. Both are now healthy; that is the next thing to chase.
+        });
 
     });
 
